From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 00:10:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] riscv64: silence newer-GCC default errors in netlib LAPACK

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [quay.io/pypa manylinux_2_39_riscv64 and musllinux_1_2_riscv64 ship a GCC that errors by default on code this old LAPACK reference C predates]

Both libcs fail identically, deep in OpenBLAS's bundled netlib LAPACK
reference sources (confirmed from real riscv64 CI logs, PR #4888),
after the TARGET=RISCV64_GENERIC fix lets the build reach that far:

    spstrf.c:830:43: error: passing argument 1 of 'dmaxloc_' from
    incompatible pointer type [-Wincompatible-pointer-types]
            itemp = mymaxloc_(&work[1], &i__4, &i__5, &c__1);
    spstrf.c:259:37: note: in definition of macro 'mymaxloc_'
    #define mymaxloc_(w,s,e,n) dmaxloc_(w,*(s),*(e),n)

spstrf.c/spstf2.c's single-precision mymaxloc_ macro has always called
the double-precision dmaxloc_ on a float* - a pre-existing bug in this
vendored, decades-old f2c-translated LAPACK reference code, harmless
under every older GCC this project has ever built with (a mere
-Wincompatible-pointer-types warning), but GCC turned that warning into
a hard error by default a few releases ago. Nothing about this is
riscv64/TARGET-specific; it would hit any architecture built with a GCC
new enough, which neither this release's x86_64/aarch64/etc targets nor
RISE's own GitLab pipeline that last built this line ever used.

Scoped to $plat == riscv64 only, so every other platform's CFLAGS (and
whichever GCC built them) stay untouched.
---
 tools/build_steps.sh | 3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

diff --git a/tools/build_steps.sh b/tools/build_steps.sh
index 316f351..d708c46 100644
--- a/tools/build_steps.sh
+++ b/tools/build_steps.sh
@@ -154,6 +154,9 @@ function do_build_lib {
             ;;
         *) echo "Strange plat value $plat"; exit 1 ;;
     esac
+    if [ "$plat" == "riscv64" ]; then
+        CFLAGS="$CFLAGS -Wno-error=incompatible-pointer-types -Wno-error=int-conversion -Wno-error=implicit-function-declaration"
+    fi
     case $interface64 in
         1)
             local interface_flags="INTERFACE64=1 SYMBOLSUFFIX=64_ LIBNAMESUFFIX=64_ OBJCONV=$PWD/objconv/objconv";
